Connect the Wi-Fi antenna: hold the RF switch at one throw

The Echo stayed on 2.4 GHz with the access point two metres away through one
wooden wall. It heard that AP at -84 dBm on 2.4 GHz and -93 on 5 GHz, and the
AP heard it at -74 - about 30 dB short in both directions, on both bands, with
every neighbouring network just as faint. The 5 GHz preference (+30 dB above
-82 dBm) could not help with nothing above -82.

MSDC2_CMD and MSDC2_CLK, GPIO85 and GPIO86, are ANT_SEL0 and ANT_SEL1: the
control lines of the RF switch in front of the MT6625L. Stock's consys
default pin state muxes them to that function; this tree never mentioned
them, so they stay at reset, inputs with pull-downs, and the switch sits at
00, which connects neither throw. Both bands then reach the radio only by
leakage through the open switch.

Driving the lines by hand on the same spot, scanning each state:

  ANT_SEL1/0   2.4 GHz   5 GHz
  00           -84       -93    (what this tree leaves)
  01           -76       -73
  10           -79       -75
  11           -84       -92

Muxing them to ANT_SEL as stock does, with the Wi-Fi module reloaded after,
changed nothing - in that function they still read 00 - so this holds them
as GPIOs at 01, the better throw on both bands, with a pin-controller hog.
That is in place as soon as the controller probes, well before the WMT
bootstrap runs its RF calibration and before Wi-Fi first scans.

Ruled out on the way, each without any change in signal: the missing stock
NVRAM (the stock WIFI file decodes to the driver's built-in defaults), the
driver's antenna_select switch (0, 1, auto), and the regulatory domain.

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts
index 2c000d0..526137c 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts
@@ -916,3 +916,45 @@
 		ecc-size = <16>;
 	};
 };
+
+/*
+ * The Wi-Fi antenna switch.
+ *
+ * MSDC2_CMD and MSDC2_CLK (GPIO85/86) are ANT_SEL0/1, the two control lines
+ * of the RF switch between the MT6625L and the antenna. Nothing in this tree
+ * set them, so they stayed at reset - inputs, pulled down - and the switch
+ * sat at 00, which connects neither throw: both bands then reach the radio
+ * only by leakage. With the Echo two metres from the AP through one wooden
+ * wall it saw 2.4 GHz at -84 dBm and 5 GHz at -93, and the AP heard it at
+ * -74. Driving the lines one-hot measured, on the same spot:
+ *
+ *	ANT_SEL1/0	2.4 GHz	5 GHz
+ *	00		-84	-93
+ *	01		-76	-73
+ *	10		-79	-75
+ *	11		-84	-92
+ *
+ * Stock muxes both pins to ANT_SEL in the consys default state and lets
+ * the connectivity block drive them, but in that function they read 00 here
+ * too. So hold them as GPIOs at 01, the better throw on both bands. It is a
+ * hog of the pin controller itself, so it is in place as soon as the
+ * controller probes, before the WMT bootstrap runs its RF calibration.
+ */
+&pio {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&wifi_ant_sel>;
+
+	wifi_ant_sel: wifi-ant-sel {
+		pins_ant_sel0 {
+			pinmux = <MT8163_PIN_85_MSDC2_CMD__FUNC_GPIO85>;
+			bias-disable;
+			output-high;
+		};
+
+		pins_ant_sel1 {
+			pinmux = <MT8163_PIN_86_MSDC2_CLK__FUNC_GPIO86>;
+			bias-disable;
+			output-low;
+		};
+	};
+};
